ctions ψα ∈ PSH(ω|Xα ) with\nψα ≤ ϕ|Xαan is equivalent to that of ψ ∈ PSH(π ? ω) with ψ ≤ π ? ϕ, and (7.10) implies that\nthe right-hand side of (8.3) coincides with Eπ? ω (π ? ϕ). Since π ? PSH(ω) ⊂ PSH(π ? ω), (7.10)\nfurther yields Eω (ϕ) ≤ Eπ? ω (π ? ϕ).\n   Conversely, pick ψ ∈ PSH(π ? ω) such that ψ ≤ π ? ϕ. After replacing ψ with max{ψ, c}\nfor a constant c ≤ ϕ, we may assume that ψ is bounded. Choose ωX ∈ Amp(X) and\nϕX ∈ PSH(ωX ) as in Theorem 4.32. Replacing ωX and ϕX with small enough multiples,\nwe may assume ωX ≤ ω, and hence ϕX ∈ PSH(ω). Since ϕ and ψ are both bounded, we\nmay further arrange, after adding a constant to ϕX , that ϕX ≤ ϕ and π ? ϕX ≤ ψ. For each\nm ∈ Z>0 , we get ϕm ∈ PSH(ω) such that\n                                   π ? ϕm = (1 − m\n                                                 1      1 ?\n                                                   )ψ + m π ϕX .\nSince ϕX ≤ ϕ, we have π ? ϕm ≤ π ? ϕ, and hence ϕm ≤ ϕ, see Theorem 4.22. On the\nother hand, since π ? ϕX ≤ ψ, Theorem 4.22 shows that (ϕm ) is an increasing sequence\nin PSH(ω), such that π ? ϕm → ψ pointwise on Y div . Now set c := min{inf ϕ, inf ψ} and\nϕ0m := max{ϕm , c}. Then (ϕ0m ) is also an increasing sequence, this time in E ∞ (ω) ⊂ E 1 (ω),\nwith π ? ϕ0m → ψ pointwise on Y div . Further, ϕ0m ≤ ϕ, and hence\n                         X\n                            cα Eω|Xα (ϕ0m |Xαan ) = Eω (ϕ0m ) ≤ Eω (ϕ),\n                            α\n\f96                         SÉBASTIEN BOUCKSOM AND MATTIAS JONSSON\n\nby (7.10). Since ϕ0m |Xαan → ψ|Xαan pointwise on Xαdiv , Theorem 7.38 yields Eω|Xα (ϕ0m |Xαan ) →\nEω|Xα (ψ|Xαan ), and hence\n                                       X\n                           Eπ? ω (ψ) =    cα Eω|Xα (ψ|Xαan ) ≤ Eω (ϕ).\n                                        α\nTaking the supremum over all ψ, we conclude, as desired, Eπ? ω (π ? ϕ) ≤ Eω (ϕ).               \u0003\n8.2. Further extensions. Using the extension of the Monge–Ampère energy E to continu-\nous functions, we now go further and extend it to (upper or lower) semicontinuous functions.\nDefinition 8.2. For any ϕ : X an → R ∪ {±∞} we set\n                       E↑ (ϕ) := sup E(ψ) | ψ ∈ C0 (X), ψ ≤ ϕ\n                                    \b\n                                                                                           (8.5)\nand\n                            E↓ (ϕ) := inf E(ψ) | ψ ∈ C0 (X), ψ ≥ ϕ .\n                                         \b\n                                                                                           (8.6)\n   While E↑ and E↓ are defined for arbitrary functions, they are mainly of interest when\nrestricted to lsc and usc functions, respectively.\nProposition 8.3. The functionals E↑ and E↓ satisfy the following properties:\n   (i) they are increasing, concave, and satisfy the algebraic properties of Proposition 8.1 (ii);\n  (ii) for any ϕ : X an → R ∪ {±∞},\n                                    E↑ (ϕ) ≤ E(ϕ) ≤ E↓ (ϕ),\n           and E↑ (ϕ) > −∞ (resp. E↓ (ϕ) < +∞) iff ϕ is bounded below (resp. bounded above);\n     (iii) we have\n                      E↑ (ϕ) = E(Q(ϕ)) = sup {E(ψ) | ρ ∈ CPSH, ψ ≤ ϕ} ;\n         we further have\n                                  E↑ (ϕ) = E(ϕ? ) = E(P(ϕ? ))\n          if ϕ is bounded below;\n     (iv) if ϕ is bounded above, then\n                                        E↓ (ϕ) = E↓ (ϕ? )\n         we further have\n                                   E↓ (ϕ) = E (ϕ? ) = E (P(ϕ? ))                           (8.7)\n                  0\n         if ϕ ∈ C (X), or ϕ ∈ PSH(ω), and for any ϕ bounded above if the envelope property\n         holds for ω;\n     (v) E↑ (resp. E↓ ) is continuous along increasing (resp. decreasing) nets of bounded-below,\n         lsc (resp. bounded above, usc) functions.\nProof. (i) and (ii) are obvious. We prove (iii). For any ψ ∈ C0 (X), we have P(ψ) = Q(ψ)\nby Lemma 5.19, and (8.2) yields\n                E(ψ) = E(P(ψ)) = E(Q(ψ)) = sup {E(ρ) | ρ ∈ CPSH, ρ ≤ ψ} .\nThis implies in turn\n  E↑ (ϕ) = sup E(ψ) | ψ ∈ C0 (X), ψ ≤ ϕ = sup {E(ρ) | ρ ∈ CPSH, ρ ≤ ϕ} = E(Q(ϕ)),\n                \b\n\nby monotonicity of E. If ϕ is bounded below, then Q(ϕ) = P(ϕ? ) by Lemma 5.19, and hence\nE(Q(ϕ)) = E(P(ϕ? )) = E(ϕ? ), which proves (iii).\n\f            GLOBAL PLURIPOTENTIAL THEORY OVER A TRIVIALLY VALUED FIELD                               97\n\n  Next we show that E↑ is continuous along increasing nets of bounded-below, lsc functions.\nLet thus (ϕi ) be an increasing net of such functions, converging to the lsc function ϕ =\nsupi ϕi . On the one hand, ϕ ≥ ϕi implies\n                           E↑ (ϕ) ≥ S := sup E↑ (ϕi ) = lim E↑ (ϕi ).\n                                             i                   i\n\nOn the other hand, for each ε > 0 we can find ψ ∈ C0 (X) such that ψ ≤ ϕ and E(ψ) ≥\nE↑ (ϕ)S− ε. Setting, for each i, Vi := {ψ < ϕi + ε} defines an increasing family of open sets\nwith i Vi = X an . By compactness, we get Vi = X an for all i large enough, i.e. ψ ≤ ϕi + ε\non X an , and hence E(ψ) ≤ E↑ (ϕi ) + ε ≤ S + ε. We thus have E↑ (ϕ) ≤ S + 2ε for all ε > 0,\nand we get, as desired, E↑ (ϕ) = S.\n   The proof that E↓ is continuous along decreasing nets of bounded-above usc functions is\nentirely similar; hence (v).\n   If ϕ ∈ C0 (X) then (8.7) follows from (8.2). If ϕ ∈ PSH(ω), write it as the limit of a\ndecreasing net in CPSH(ω). Then E(ϕi ) → E↓ (ϕ), by (v), and E(ϕi ) → E(ϕ), by continuity\nof E along decreasing nets in PSH(ω). This proves (8.7) in that case.\n   Now consider the general case, assuming the envelope property. Write ϕ? as the decreasing\nlimit of a net (ϕi ) in C0 (X). For each i we have E↓ (ϕi ) = E(P(ϕi )). On the one hand,\nE↓ (ϕi ) → E↓ (ϕ), by (v). On the other hand, Corollary 5.18 implies that P(ϕi ) decreases\nto P(ϕ? ) in PSH(ω), and hence E(P(ϕi )) → E(P(ϕ? )) = E(ϕ? ), by continuity of E along\ndecreasing nets in PSH(ω) and (8.2). This concludes the proof of (iv).                     \u0003\nCorolla